A collector combines diffusion, interception and inertia (@cm-collector: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @clean-manufacturing, #source "../sources/Fluid-Mechanics-of-Aerosols-Ahmadi.lingua#r_175C6Q2GTKPZJJ6XSRHF5979G0", #source "../sources/Air-Cleaning-Handbook-DOE.lingua#r_09QPEZ76E9W6HS7R55X2K8TB3X", #clean-manufacturing "../../../physics/fluid-dynamics/turbulence/Reynolds-averaging-stress-transport-and-the-closure-problem.lingua#r_2WR3KYF31R7RJSN20P6PKS0PM0") { r_5FKV7DDSNF79HVKFBGXYBP5TTR
**Read:** [The fluid mechanics of aerosols — Goodarz Ahmadi, Clarkson](../sources/Fluid-Mechanics-of-Aerosols-Ahmadi.lingua#r_175C6Q2GTKPZJJ6XSRHF5979G0), Diffusion to a Cylinder and Inertia Impactions in full; [Air-cleaning systems — DOE-HDBK-1169-2022](../sources/Air-Cleaning-Handbook-DOE.lingua#r_09QPEZ76E9W6HS7R55X2K8TB3X), filter collection and retention.

Diffusion brings wandering particles across streamlines; finite size allows contact when a particle centre follows a streamline near a surface; inertia creates lag as the fluid turns. Their contributions depend on collector geometry, particle properties and flow. A single mechanism's asymptotic efficiency cannot always be added to another without counting some captured particles twice.

Efficiency requires a denominator: particles reaching a defined upstream area, entering a device or passing through its medium. Define it before comparing a single-fiber theory with a complete filter, bypass path or instrument sampler. Retention after contact is another part of the result.

**Work:** Reproduce one limiting collector calculation from the notes with its exact radius/diameter conventions. Compare it with a different mechanism and identify the overlap of assumptions. Explain what an installed-device experiment would need to establish beyond the ideal collector calculation.

